      'Speed and width carry emotion. A slow, wide vibrato sounds like lament or weight; a fast, narrow one sounds urgent or nervous; a delayed vibrato (the note starts straight and then begins to move) sounds like a singer leaning into a word. Violinists went through a long debate about this: in the early twentieth century continuous vibrato became normal, while older practice used it as an ornament on chosen notes. Guitarists face the same choice. Vibrato on every note is like shouting every word; vibrato on the right notes makes them matter.',
      'The body lesson is that vibrato does not come from the finger. If you try to wobble the string by flexing the fingertip, the motion is small, tense and uneven. The power comes from the forearm rotating, as if turning a doorknob, or from the wrist, with the fingertip acting only as the point of contact. A good vibrato feels like the hand is breathing; a bad one feels like the hand is shivering.',
      'Finally, vibrato is a time skill. A controlled vibrato has a rate you could tap your foot to. Some players lock the rate to the tempo of the song, others let it float. Either way, you should be able to choose it, and the way to choose it is to practise it slowly, measured against a click, until the wobble is something you do rather than something that happens to you.'
